WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.620
- Governors have mandated
generally, not all of them,

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:04.960
but most of them have said

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.261
there will be no elective surgeries.

00:00:06.261 --> 00:00:08.461
Because we need to clear the hospitals

00:00:08.461 --> 00:00:12.362
so that we can have beds
and equipment for people

00:00:12.362 --> 00:00:15.800
with respiratory failure because of COVID.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.031
Well, the unintended consequence of that

00:00:18.031 --> 00:00:20.782
is that cancer patients
are forgoing treatment.

00:00:20.782 --> 00:00:25.780
And a lot of hospitals are
on the verge of bankruptcy

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:26.991
because elective surgery

00:00:26.991 --> 00:00:29.140
was a key part of their economic model.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:30.552
So they've made things worse

00:00:30.552 --> 00:00:32.676
by trying to make things better.

00:00:32.676 --> 00:00:35.259
(upbeat music)

00:00:41.742 --> 00:00:43.910
- Hey, I'm Dave Rubin and
according to the paperwork,

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:45.560
this is still the Rubin Report.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.500
Friendly reminder, everybody to subscribe

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:48.780
to our YouTube channel

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:51.196
and click that pesky notification bell.

00:00:51.196 --> 00:00:54.010
Joining me today is an author, a speaker,

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:57.860
and the host of Kibbe on Liberty
on the Blaze, Matt Kibbe.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:00.189
Finally, welcome to the Rubin Report.

00:01:00.189 --> 00:01:01.650
- Finally, hey Dave.

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.090
How's it going?

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:04.350
- It's going all right.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:06.590
I have been on your show several times

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:09.134
and we were overdue to do it this way.

00:01:09.134 --> 00:01:12.436
Flip the interviewer,
interviewee situation.

00:01:12.436 --> 00:01:14.226
Every time I go on your show,

00:01:14.226 --> 00:01:16.390
we spend about 80% of it talking

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:18.230
about Star Wars and Liberty.

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:20.429
So I feel like that's sort of
where we have to start here.

00:01:20.429 --> 00:01:22.766
I'll just tell my audience right up front,

00:01:22.766 --> 00:01:24.200
you're a big Liberty guy.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.030
For those that don't know
you, you're a libertarian.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:28.340
You've worked for Rand Paul,

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:30.410
you run a nonprofit, Free The People,

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:33.440
that spreads the ideas
of liberty and freedom.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.770
We're gonna get far more into all of that

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:38.024
obviously related to
Coronavirus and everything else.

00:01:38.024 --> 00:01:41.675
But I feel that I have to at
least allow you to pontificate

00:01:41.675 --> 00:01:45.376
on Star Wars and explain a
little bit of how Star Wars,

00:01:45.376 --> 00:01:47.740
at least the prequels, and the originals

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.570
do have a Liberty flair

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:52.130
that a lot of people don't know about.

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:53.730
- You know, per your recommendation

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:56.690
I actually went back
after the last show we did

00:01:56.690 --> 00:01:58.254
and I rewatched the prequels

00:01:58.254 --> 00:02:01.060
and I'll probably get
slaughtered for this,

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:01.893
but I think you're right.

00:02:01.893 --> 00:02:05.545
I think the prequels may in
fact be the most relevant

00:02:05.545 --> 00:02:09.149
and they may stand up to the test of time.

00:02:09.149 --> 00:02:13.090
I can count at least 25, 30 governors

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.622
that want to be Palpatine right now.

00:02:14.622 --> 00:02:15.455
(Dave laughs loudly)

00:02:15.455 --> 00:02:18.340
So, which we should probably all go back

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:20.880
and watch this and see exactly

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:23.320
how freedom dies to thunderous applause.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:26.540
Because I think we're
living this in real-time,

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:30.010
and all of these things
that governors are doing

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:32.022
seemed like fundamentally anti-democratic,

00:02:32.022 --> 00:02:34.991
and it was all predicted in the prequels.

00:02:34.991 --> 00:02:37.410
- It was all, and Jar Jar Binks,

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:40.661
who does that make Jar Jar
Binks in a modern sense.

00:02:40.661 --> 00:02:45.099
- I don't know, I even
disliked him, less dislike him,

00:02:45.099 --> 00:02:45.932
(Dave laughs softly)

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:48.192
dislike him less than I did before.

00:02:48.192 --> 00:02:53.192
Which is shocking to me because
I just, I couldn't early on,

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:56.261
but I'm even pro Jar Jar at this point.

00:02:56.261 --> 00:03:00.380
- Yeah, and probably dislike him less than

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.588
the way you dislike some
of the current governors,

00:03:02.588 --> 00:03:04.301
also, I would imagine.

00:03:04.301 --> 00:03:06.790
- Yeah, I mean, but that's a low bar.

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:09.090
So I'm giving Jar Jar more than that.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:11.450
- Okay, so putting aside Star Wars,

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:13.100
although maybe we can
sort of weave it back

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.062
into this whole thing.

00:03:14.062 --> 00:03:17.690
For those that don't know
you give me a little bit

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:19.960
of Matt Kibbe 101 background.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:22.983
What got you into the Liberty
scene in the first place?

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:28.410
- So I was, my gateway drug to Liberty

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:31.390
was actually an album
by a band called Rush,

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:33.300
it's called 2112.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.070
And you'll talk to a
number of libertarians,

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:37.379
particularly libertarians my
age, will tell you this story.

00:03:37.379 --> 00:03:40.168
It was dedicated to
the genius of Ayn Rand.

00:03:40.168 --> 00:03:42.210
I was 13 years old.

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:44.928
I had no idea who that dude Ayn Rand was,

00:03:44.928 --> 00:03:47.689
but she had such an interesting name.

00:03:47.689 --> 00:03:51.710
I went out and stumbled across an old copy

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:54.649
of her novella Anthem and I devoured it.

00:03:54.649 --> 00:03:56.829
And I followed the breadcrumbs.

00:03:56.829 --> 00:04:00.010
And eventually, Ayn Rand, if
you read her nonfiction stuff,

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:02.271
she says to read the Austrian economists

00:04:02.271 --> 00:04:04.770
and specifically, Ludwig von Mises.

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:07.450
So I started reading all
that stuff when I was a kid.

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:10.150
And if nothing else,
it was the perfect way

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:13.680
to never get a date because
I couldn't get girls

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.300
that wanted to talk
about Austrian economics.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:16.514
It's kind of strange.

00:04:16.514 --> 00:04:18.223
- That's very bizarre.

00:04:18.223 --> 00:04:21.938
Maybe you were doing it wrong somehow.

00:04:21.938 --> 00:04:25.797
- Yeah, maybe, but I ended
up coming to the Death Star,

00:04:25.797 --> 00:04:29.410
the belly of the beast, Washington, DC.

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:31.837
You go to graduate school
at George Mason University

00:04:31.837 --> 00:04:35.969
to study economics and
eventually as it always does,

00:04:35.969 --> 00:04:38.271
politics sort of sucks you in.

00:04:38.271 --> 00:04:42.379
And I have all sorts of
horrible things in my past.

00:04:42.379 --> 00:04:45.520
I was the Chief Economist for Lee Atwater

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:47.630
at the Republican National Committee.

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:49.890
I was budget director at
the US Chamber of Commerce

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:52.269
and eventually, I went on to found

00:04:52.269 --> 00:04:55.300
a tea party organization
called FreedomWorks.

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:57.630
And that was ancient history now.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:01.250
But I went from economist
to political hack

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:06.250
to grassroots organizer and
now hopefully communicator.

00:05:06.251 --> 00:05:09.365
- Yeah, what actually interests you more?

00:05:09.365 --> 00:05:12.020
I guess economics
fundamentally interests you

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:14.900
more than politics per se
because now we're very much

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:16.860
in the same space where we're sharing

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:19.130
the ideas of Liberty obviously.

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:20.250
But you have more of sort of

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:24.348
a schooling background in economics.

00:05:24.348 --> 00:05:27.520
- Yeah, I mean, the
thing that I wanted to do

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:29.310
when I was a kid was write.

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:30.837
And I spent a lot of time writing.

00:05:30.837 --> 00:05:34.180
I was one of those
awkward libertarian kids.

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:36.900
So you can imagine that
anything that doesn't involve

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:39.207
social interaction was
pretty attractive to me.

00:05:39.207 --> 00:05:41.018
And I wanted to be a writer.

00:05:41.018 --> 00:05:45.610
And I started off writing very
academic stuff and you know,

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:47.178
if you were lucky you could get 10 people

00:05:47.178 --> 00:05:50.820
to read your peer-reviewed
article with all the footnotes,

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:54.257
and all the fancy words that
we academics like to use

00:05:54.257 --> 00:05:56.750
to pretend that we know more than we do.

00:05:56.750 --> 00:05:58.455
But I wrote my first
Wall Street Journal piece

00:05:58.455 --> 00:06:01.560
and I just did the math and
I realized, you know what,

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:03.908
if I could learn how to
communicate in plain English,

00:06:03.908 --> 00:06:07.000
I could probably reach
a much broader audience.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.880
And that's definitely
what we tried to do today.

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:11.900
We don't do a lot of
writing and free the people,

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:15.648
but we use video storytelling
to try to sort of

00:06:15.648 --> 00:06:18.998
bridge the language divide between people

00:06:18.998 --> 00:06:22.880
that would identify with
different political tribes.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:26.740
Because I do believe that
Liberty is a human value.

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:28.326
It's built into our systems

00:06:28.326 --> 00:06:31.690
and if we could figure out
a way to listen to people

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:33.150
that are sort of Liberty curious,

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:34.700
and try to connect with them,

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:37.947
I think that would grow
our community in a way

00:06:37.947 --> 00:06:41.977
that just speaking
economics will never do.

00:06:41.977 --> 00:06:44.220
- Yeah, I should mention, by the way,

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:45.770
we've already been
talking for a few minutes

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:47.578
and I did not point out
that for a libertarian

00:06:47.578 --> 00:06:49.610
you're dressed fairly well.

00:06:49.610 --> 00:06:51.027
You know you go to a
libertarian conference,

00:06:51.027 --> 00:06:54.590
you got naked people, you
got people with crazy shirts.

00:06:54.590 --> 00:06:56.007
Look at you, I mean you have a tie on,

00:06:56.007 --> 00:06:58.670
your jacket and shirt
seemed to fit appropriately.

00:06:58.670 --> 00:07:01.060
Are you sure you're hanging
with the right crew?

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:01.893
What's going on over there?

00:07:01.893 --> 00:07:05.960
- This is kind of a forest
quarantine counter-revolution.

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:10.913
Because I've worn all
of my concert t-shirts,

00:07:10.913 --> 00:07:14.020
and I haven't showered for days,

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:17.680
but I decided that if I'm gonna
start breaking quarantine,

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:20.685
which is where I'm at
now, I need to look good.

00:07:20.685 --> 00:07:23.010
The problem is I still
don't have pants on.

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:25.330
So it's gonna somehow, it's
probably going to be awkward

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:26.860
if I go outside.

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:28.736
- You and me both
brother, you and me both.

00:07:28.736 --> 00:07:31.050
But let's not this is a family show,

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:32.317
so we'll leave it at that.

00:07:32.317 --> 00:07:34.341
Okay, so I think having you on now

00:07:34.341 --> 00:07:36.565
was sort of the perfect time because

00:07:36.565 --> 00:07:40.957
if Liberty ever needed a
jolt or if it ever was about

00:07:40.957 --> 00:07:43.808
to have a resurgence, I
think both of those things

00:07:43.808 --> 00:07:45.720
are sort of possible right now.

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:47.940
I sense that we need it more than ever,

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:49.660
at least in modern times right now.

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:51.420
And that it could be on the cusp

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:54.150
of really gaining momentum right now.

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:57.746
So give me your basic breakdown
of sort of where we're at

00:07:57.746 --> 00:08:01.740
with Corona, sort of what you
thought we should have done.

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:04.587
What was the appropriate
Liberty-minded response,

00:08:04.587 --> 00:08:06.870
say two months ago in this first thing

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:10.491
was rolling out to kind
of where we are right now.

00:08:10.491 --> 00:08:14.120
- So my initial reaction
and I went back and checked,

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:17.430
the first piece I wrote about the pandemic

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:19.160
was I think March 16th.

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:21.488
So pretty early on I actually wrote it

00:08:21.488 --> 00:08:24.473
on a beach in Jost Van
Dyke, I was on vacation

00:08:24.473 --> 00:08:26.015
and we had to cancel it.

00:08:26.015 --> 00:08:28.280
To get back to the United States

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:30.485
before I was afraid they were
gonna close the airports.

00:08:30.485 --> 00:08:34.310
And me being a geek, I wrote this piece.

00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:38.330
But I was worried that
centralized top-down

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:41.900
one size fits all solutions,
the very complex problems

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:46.129
was inevitably going to
create a lot of unintended

00:08:46.129 --> 00:08:48.370
and destructive consequences.

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:51.280
And I was thinking about
one of my economist heroes

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:54.484
Frederic Bastiat, and he talks about

00:08:54.484 --> 00:08:56.256
the seen and the unseen.

00:08:56.256 --> 00:09:00.746
And the inability of people to conceive of

00:09:00.746 --> 00:09:03.140
the incredibly complex division of labor

00:09:03.140 --> 00:09:05.140
that brings food to your table every day.

00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:06.870
You don't know how it happened

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:08.940
and you can see that playing out now.

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:12.140
People just assumed
that food would show up.

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:14.466
And so when grocery
store shelves are empty,

00:09:14.466 --> 00:09:16.530
they start panicking

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:18.630
and frankly, they start blaming markets

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:22.190
when in fact it was meddling
into the market process

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:23.137
that created that.

00:09:23.137 --> 00:09:26.763
And so not even conceiving
that we would do

00:09:26.763 --> 00:09:30.370
these massive never-ending lockdowns

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:32.132
the way the governors did.

00:09:32.132 --> 00:09:34.910
I was worried that the
government was going to do

00:09:34.910 --> 00:09:37.151
all sorts of things that
would make it more difficult

00:09:37.151 --> 00:09:41.360
to deal with not just the
disease and the virus.

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:43.158
But all the other things we need to do

00:09:43.158 --> 00:09:45.705
to keep our families safe.

00:09:45.705 --> 00:09:48.278
And one of those things is earning income.

00:09:48.278 --> 00:09:50.101
Another is put food on the table.

00:09:50.101 --> 00:09:54.620
A third is actually being
able to take care of yourself

00:09:54.620 --> 00:09:57.720
and go outside maybe get cancer treatments

00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:01.390
and all these things that are
now proving impossible to do.

00:10:01.390 --> 00:10:03.300
So knowing the government fails,

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:06.630
I was afraid that they were
gonna fail philosophy bad.

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:08.623
And that's kind of where we're at.

00:10:08.623 --> 00:10:11.499
- Okay, so I think most of
my audience will be with you

00:10:11.499 --> 00:10:16.499
on the, "Okay, we don't like
the top-down version of this.

00:10:16.597 --> 00:10:18.627
"We want to figure out sort of local-level

00:10:18.627 --> 00:10:20.750
"individual stuff first."

00:10:20.750 --> 00:10:23.109
What I see a lot of people
on the Twitter saying is,

00:10:23.109 --> 00:10:27.437
"Oh, this shows the
flaw of libertarianism.

00:10:27.437 --> 00:10:29.857
"This shows the flaw of small government

00:10:29.857 --> 00:10:33.225
"because, in a time like this,
a pandemic knows no borders.

00:10:33.225 --> 00:10:37.257
"We need strong national decisions
that are across the board

00:10:37.257 --> 00:10:38.450
"and the rest of it."

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:40.556
How do you combat that concept?

00:10:40.556 --> 00:10:45.556
- Well, I think the entire
purpose of the market process,

00:10:45.573 --> 00:10:47.760
and I call it a mutual support system.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:51.610
The entire purpose of
people getting together

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:55.038
and cooperating and trading
and engaging in ideas

00:10:55.038 --> 00:11:00.038
is because we live in a
radically uncertain world.

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:02.260
What happened yesterday
is in no way tells us

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:03.880
what's gonna happen tomorrow.

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:05.550
And the whole point for that process

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:08.670
is for people to figure
out really complex things

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:09.860
that are going to happen to us.

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:12.270
And it feels like this exogenous shock,

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:15.149
this natural disaster of a global pandemic

00:11:15.149 --> 00:11:18.387
is exactly what you want to
decentralize system to solve.

00:11:18.387 --> 00:11:20.375
And on top on that,

00:11:20.375 --> 00:11:24.001
how much government officials don't know.

00:11:24.001 --> 00:11:26.280
We always talk about market failure,

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:28.020
but we never talk about
government failure.

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:31.140
And you can check some of these boxes,

00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:34.070
some of the horrific things that federal

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:36.270
and state governments have
done since the beginning

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:39.200
of this pandemic to
screw up the food supply.

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:44.200
To, you remember this
supposedly cache of N95 masks.

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:46.968
- Oh yeah, oh yeah.

00:11:46.968 --> 00:11:49.660
- How many administration you gonna have?

00:11:49.660 --> 00:11:51.810
Well, guess what, government
fails, it always fails,

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:54.080
because politics is a horrible way

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:55.560
to solve complex problems.

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:57.500
So when you combine those two things,

00:11:57.500 --> 00:12:00.910
if you're gonna shut off
the ingenuity of free people

00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:02.240
to solve problems.

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:04.148
And replace it with the central plan,

00:12:04.148 --> 00:12:07.853
that is by definition going
to be dysfunctional and wrong.

00:12:07.853 --> 00:12:11.222
That's where you get
really, really bad things

00:12:11.222 --> 00:12:12.975
in our society.

00:12:12.975 --> 00:12:16.524
- Do you sense that a lot
of the divide on the people

00:12:16.524 --> 00:12:19.620
that view the world
top-down versus the people

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:20.960
that view the world bottom up.

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:24.870
That it's just a fundamental
psychological conditioning

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:25.980
that they have.

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:28.003
That it's not just, "Oh, we learned,

00:12:28.003 --> 00:12:30.802
"we have different economic
policies let's say."

00:12:30.802 --> 00:12:34.350
But that their fear centers
are different or you know,

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:36.571
I find when I hear all
the big government people.

00:12:36.571 --> 00:12:39.912
They seem to have this trust in a thing

00:12:39.912 --> 00:12:42.330
just because the thing exists.

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:44.440
So it's sort of inherently must be good

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:47.974
because it existed or maybe
50 years ago it was better

00:12:47.974 --> 00:12:50.170
or something like that.

00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:53.160
Versus I think people more
in line with our thinking,

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:54.826
where it's like, "Well,
just because it exists,

00:12:54.826 --> 00:12:57.377
"it doesn't mean it's inherently good.

00:12:57.377 --> 00:12:59.020
"So I want to just scale the power back."

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:01.300
Do you think that how much
a certain amount of this

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:03.793
is just your psychological makeup?

00:13:03.793 --> 00:13:07.001
- You know, it may be that

00:13:07.001 --> 00:13:10.743
and not to sort of castigate
my progressive friends.

00:13:10.743 --> 00:13:15.743
But I think people don't appreciate

00:13:16.085 --> 00:13:19.630
just how much we don't
know as individuals.

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:22.495
And how much our leaders don't know,

00:13:22.495 --> 00:13:25.249
even though they went to
all the right colleges

00:13:25.249 --> 00:13:27.850
and have all of these credentials.

00:13:27.850 --> 00:13:31.141
And so maybe there's a line
in the sand about humility

00:13:31.141 --> 00:13:36.141
and being willing to say in
public what is this new pandemic

00:13:36.260 --> 00:13:38.717
and how does it compare to past pandemics

00:13:38.717 --> 00:13:40.835
and what don't we know?

00:13:40.835 --> 00:13:43.570
If some of these modelers would have said,

00:13:43.570 --> 00:13:46.260
you know what, there's no
way that I could model this

00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:47.700
in an honest way.

00:13:47.700 --> 00:13:49.450
So let me tell you the
things that we do know.

00:13:49.450 --> 00:13:52.263
But let me also tell you
that if I project numbers,

00:13:52.263 --> 00:13:54.210
there's no way it's going to be right.

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:55.430
It's always going to be wrong.

00:13:55.430 --> 00:13:58.244
And that sort of goes
back to the old dream

00:13:58.244 --> 00:14:00.530
of a progressivism.

00:14:00.530 --> 00:14:03.081
That you could take science
and science was settled

00:14:03.081 --> 00:14:06.250
and really smart people that
went to the right colleges

00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:09.360
could take that science and
impose it on the rest of us.

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:12.054
And we would have this
sort of rationally planned

00:14:12.054 --> 00:14:13.873
top-down society.

00:14:13.873 --> 00:14:15.642
But that lacks humility.

00:14:15.642 --> 00:14:20.642
It lacks any sense that you
couldn't possibly know enough

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:22.630
to plan a complex economy.

00:14:22.630 --> 00:14:25.640
And this was Rhetoric
Hayek's, entire critique

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:27.420
of socialist planning.

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:30.980
And I think it applies
to any would-be planner,

00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:35.700
even some of the governors
and how they're telling us

00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:37.823
all in a very microcosmic way

00:14:37.823 --> 00:14:39.993
like you should do this
and you shouldn't do that.

00:14:39.993 --> 00:14:41.637
- Yeah, we talked about this a little bit

00:14:41.637 --> 00:14:43.220
when I was on your show
a couple of weeks ago,

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:45.750
but do you think some of
this sort of also is just

00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:48.140
the end of secularism?

00:14:48.140 --> 00:14:50.250
In a weird way, it's the end of just,

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:53.210
Oh, people, just creating
systems in the moment

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:54.790
that aren't necessarily experts

00:14:54.790 --> 00:14:56.440
and that the experts get everything wrong.

00:14:56.440 --> 00:14:58.340
And that we just keep sort of outsourcing

00:14:58.340 --> 00:14:59.460
all of our decision making.

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:01.570
And every day we wake
up and as you're saying

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:05.164
yesterday doesn't really
form what tomorrow will be.

00:15:05.164 --> 00:15:07.040
And that we've just sort of hit the end

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:10.910
of what we can build in a weird way.

00:15:10.910 --> 00:15:12.315
Something like that.

00:15:12.315 --> 00:15:16.567
- Yeah, like there's definitely
been this democratization

00:15:16.567 --> 00:15:19.780
and decentralization, disintermediation.

00:15:19.780 --> 00:15:20.921
My wife hates it when I use that word.

00:15:20.921 --> 00:15:23.390
Because it doesn't mean
anything to anybody,

00:15:23.390 --> 00:15:28.390
but technology has democratized
knowledge and information.

00:15:28.604 --> 00:15:32.260
And I happen to be one of those
guys that was super romantic

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:35.630
about the potential to
decentralize everything

00:15:35.630 --> 00:15:36.482
based on that.

00:15:36.482 --> 00:15:39.760
But it seems like there's a backlash now.

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:43.255
And you have clickbait
and you have censorship

00:15:43.255 --> 00:15:45.500
and you have all of these things

00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:47.610
where sort of the empire striking back

00:15:47.610 --> 00:15:50.410
to get back to that phase.

00:15:50.410 --> 00:15:51.243
- I knew you'd get there.

00:15:51.243 --> 00:15:52.710
I knew you'd get there.

00:15:52.710 --> 00:15:54.706
- The experts do not like the fact

00:15:54.706 --> 00:15:58.080
that we probably don't
need to go to school

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:01.300
to figure out electrical engineering.

00:16:01.300 --> 00:16:05.330
We probably don't need to
defer to epidemiologists

00:16:05.330 --> 00:16:06.690
because we could actually do some

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:08.520
of that research for ourselves.

00:16:08.520 --> 00:16:10.930
And discover that there is
patterns and there is history

00:16:10.930 --> 00:16:12.747
and all that stuff is available to us.

00:16:12.747 --> 00:16:17.747
I think in a lot of ways
the backlash by the planners

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:21.928
is because we were
actually needing them less.

00:16:21.928 --> 00:16:24.930
And they see an opportunity right now

00:16:24.930 --> 00:16:29.170
to reconsolidate power and take it back.

00:16:29.170 --> 00:16:32.580
- So I'm very much along that,
that's what this feels to me.

00:16:32.580 --> 00:16:35.440
That they had almost
completely been decimated.

00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:38.299
We've all sort of woken
up to the idea that,

00:16:38.299 --> 00:16:40.670
you know, a four-year college

00:16:40.670 --> 00:16:42.823
that you're gonna spend 120
grand on isn't necessary.

00:16:42.823 --> 00:16:45.060
And you know, that all of these

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:46.580
giant institutions are failing.

00:16:46.580 --> 00:16:48.196
And we'll talk a little
bit more about journalism

00:16:48.196 --> 00:16:49.350
in just a sec.

00:16:49.350 --> 00:16:51.410
So you really think, do you
think though that this is

00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:54.080
some sort of coordinated move then

00:16:54.080 --> 00:16:58.140
by that establishment or
those institutions to do this?

00:16:58.140 --> 00:16:59.240
I mean, this is where we get into

00:16:59.240 --> 00:17:00.990
like some strange conspiracy stuff.

00:17:00.990 --> 00:17:03.474
But these days, anyone who's
not a conspiracy theorist

00:17:03.474 --> 00:17:04.985
isn't really thinking, you know,

00:17:04.985 --> 00:17:07.085
when you're trapped in your house all day.

00:17:08.260 --> 00:17:10.230
- Well, I don't believe
in grand conspiracies

00:17:10.230 --> 00:17:15.110
because of the natural
failures of governments

00:17:15.110 --> 00:17:15.980
and everything else.

00:17:15.980 --> 00:17:17.940
But you know, you go back to Adam Smith

00:17:17.940 --> 00:17:21.290
and I won't quote the quote
because I'll butcher it,

00:17:21.290 --> 00:17:25.100
but the natural tendency
of business people

00:17:25.100 --> 00:17:27.159
and others to conspire to hold up prices.

00:17:27.159 --> 00:17:29.302
This is part of human nature.

00:17:29.302 --> 00:17:32.620
And you don't have to
be a conspiracy theorist

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:34.660
to understand that the incentives align

00:17:34.660 --> 00:17:39.020
with all of the people
that are using this crisis

00:17:39.020 --> 00:17:41.625
to radically expand
the power of the state.

00:17:41.625 --> 00:17:45.230
You know, the collusion, and
we've talked about this before.

00:17:45.230 --> 00:17:47.657
The collusion between government experts

00:17:47.657 --> 00:17:51.323
and social media czars
who are now deciding

00:17:51.323 --> 00:17:53.830
which scientists we get to hear from,

00:17:53.830 --> 00:17:55.940
which economists, which opinions,

00:17:55.940 --> 00:17:57.212
and it's gotten really absurd.

00:17:57.212 --> 00:18:00.030
I don't think that's a grand conspiracy.

00:18:00.030 --> 00:18:02.182
I just think their incentives
align and they want control

00:18:02.182 --> 00:18:04.531
and they want control of the narrative.

00:18:04.531 --> 00:18:07.330
They want control of the market,

00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:09.460
they want control of government power,

00:18:09.460 --> 00:18:10.758
whatever it is they're trying to control.

00:18:10.758 --> 00:18:15.110
This works perfectly
because people are nervous,

00:18:15.110 --> 00:18:15.960
people are anxious.

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:18.102
There's radical uncertainty right now

00:18:18.102 --> 00:18:20.883
and people are exploiting it.

00:18:20.883 --> 00:18:23.020
- Okay, so let's back up a little bit.

00:18:23.020 --> 00:18:26.120
So with all this in mind, let's
pretend Kibbe's President,

00:18:26.120 --> 00:18:30.500
it's 2020 America has some
sort of, you know, libertarian.

00:18:30.500 --> 00:18:31.923
I don't like to say libertarian utopia

00:18:31.923 --> 00:18:34.692
because I don't think libertarians
really believe in utopia

00:18:34.692 --> 00:18:37.410
in the way that progressives
tend to believe in it.

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:39.137
But we have, you know, some sort of

00:18:39.137 --> 00:18:41.860
more liberty-minded state,

00:18:41.860 --> 00:18:43.984
may be that the founders had planned.

00:18:43.984 --> 00:18:46.380
And you're president and
you start getting all

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:50.121
these communiques and
messages about Coronavirus.

00:18:50.121 --> 00:18:52.566
Is the first thing that
you do as a president

00:18:52.566 --> 00:18:54.867
to tell all of the governors,

00:18:54.867 --> 00:18:56.507
"You guys just handle this

00:18:56.507 --> 00:18:58.987
"and when you need help you call us.

00:18:58.987 --> 00:19:00.580
"But I'm not gonna do anything with that."

00:19:00.580 --> 00:19:02.784
Would that be the sort of liberty take

00:19:02.784 --> 00:19:05.214
on what the president
should be doing right now?

00:19:05.214 --> 00:19:08.260
- It would definitely be federalism

00:19:08.260 --> 00:19:11.592
but to take it even a
step further localism

00:19:11.592 --> 00:19:14.143
and even individualism.

00:19:14.143 --> 00:19:17.960
Ayn Ryan famously said that
the discriminated minority

00:19:17.960 --> 00:19:18.942
is the individual.

00:19:18.942 --> 00:19:21.620
And I think that even at the local level,

00:19:21.620 --> 00:19:24.378
there can be tyranny if governments

00:19:24.378 --> 00:19:26.393
get too full of themselves.

00:19:26.393 --> 00:19:30.240
And the key to really complex problems,

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:31.461
things you don't know anything about

00:19:31.461 --> 00:19:36.140
is tapping into local
knowledge and problem-solving.

00:19:36.140 --> 00:19:39.020
And this is what governors
are getting wrong.

00:19:39.020 --> 00:19:41.470
So I don't think federalism
is going to solve our problem.

00:19:41.470 --> 00:19:43.840
You know that the libertarian joke

00:19:43.840 --> 00:19:46.252
is that we're conspiring
to take over the government

00:19:46.252 --> 00:19:47.800
so they'll leave you alone.

00:19:47.800 --> 00:19:52.800
And I guess that would be my
mandate if I was president,

00:19:52.910 --> 00:19:53.743
God helped me.

00:19:53.743 --> 00:19:58.253
But it gets back to the humility thing.

00:19:58.253 --> 00:20:00.764
And you see this playing
out in very practical ways.

00:20:00.764 --> 00:20:04.980
Governors have mandated,
generally, not all of them.

00:20:04.980 --> 00:20:06.670
But most of them has said there will be

00:20:06.670 --> 00:20:09.710
no elective surgeries because
we need to clear the hospitals

00:20:09.710 --> 00:20:12.890
so that we can have beds and equipment

00:20:12.890 --> 00:20:17.150
for people with respiratory
failure because of COVID.

00:20:17.150 --> 00:20:19.343
Well, the unintended consequence of that

00:20:19.343 --> 00:20:22.045
is that cancer patients
are forgoing treatment

00:20:22.045 --> 00:20:26.727
and a lot of hospitals are
on the verge of bankruptcy

00:20:26.727 --> 00:20:28.089
because elective surgery

00:20:28.089 --> 00:20:30.510
was a key part of the economic model.

00:20:30.510 --> 00:20:31.678
So they've made things worse

00:20:31.678 --> 00:20:33.772
by trying to make things better.

00:20:33.772 --> 00:20:36.060
The only people that would know for sure

00:20:36.060 --> 00:20:37.504
what the right policy would be

00:20:37.504 --> 00:20:41.571
to make sure that a hospital
was safe and well run

00:20:41.571 --> 00:20:43.053
and had capacity.

00:20:43.053 --> 00:20:45.920
Would be to let doctors and patients

00:20:45.920 --> 00:20:47.910
and hospital administrators
in that community

00:20:47.910 --> 00:20:49.514
figure that stuff out for themselves.

00:20:49.514 --> 00:20:53.140
That's gotta be the way
that we get out of this mess

00:20:53.140 --> 00:20:53.977
now that we've created it.

00:20:53.977 --> 00:20:56.691
But that would be my take.

00:20:56.691 --> 00:20:58.760
- What is the inherent problem with that,

00:20:58.760 --> 00:21:00.120
that the blue state governors

00:21:00.120 --> 00:21:02.500
and the lefties more generally?

00:21:02.500 --> 00:21:05.300
They don't view paying for these things

00:21:05.300 --> 00:21:08.340
or hospitals having to,
you know, pay people

00:21:08.340 --> 00:21:10.030
or any of these things as necessary.

00:21:10.030 --> 00:21:13.050
It's sort of, "Oh well do
whatever you're supposed to do,"

00:21:13.050 --> 00:21:16.230
as they define it and
then we'll pay for it.

00:21:16.230 --> 00:21:17.995
Meaning we'll just
magically make up numbers.

00:21:17.995 --> 00:21:21.320
So logic is just sort
of coming against like

00:21:21.320 --> 00:21:25.023
a black hole in a certain regard.

00:21:27.640 --> 00:21:31.040
- I think a lot of governors
have suddenly discovering that

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:32.940
if they close down all the businesses,

00:21:32.940 --> 00:21:35.871
there won't be any revenue
to pay for all of the--

00:21:35.871 --> 00:21:37.450
- Incredible.

00:21:37.450 --> 00:21:39.840
- And now they're of course
going to the Federal Government

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:41.120
begging for bailout there.

00:21:41.120 --> 00:21:42.650
And I hope we don't give it to them

00:21:42.650 --> 00:21:45.360
because that'll just
reward really bad behavior.

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:48.666
But there is like a
shocking naivete about,

00:21:48.666 --> 00:21:51.470
and I said this earlier, how
does food get on the table?

00:21:51.470 --> 00:21:54.322
But where does all that revenue come from

00:21:54.322 --> 00:21:56.740
that funds all of these programs?

00:21:56.740 --> 00:21:58.548
How do hospitals stay in business?

00:21:58.548 --> 00:22:03.340
It's very much a market
that allows for that

00:22:03.340 --> 00:22:04.601
where you have customers

00:22:04.601 --> 00:22:07.530
and you have people selling services.

00:22:07.530 --> 00:22:09.410
And you know, for some
reason we don't think that

00:22:09.410 --> 00:22:10.748
that's appropriate in healthcare.

00:22:10.748 --> 00:22:14.583
But it's essential if you
actually want access to a doctor

00:22:14.583 --> 00:22:16.989
when you're diagnosed with cancer.

00:22:16.989 --> 00:22:20.446
And no amount of empty government promises

00:22:20.446 --> 00:22:22.600
are a substitute for that.

00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:24.330
And we're gonna learn this right now.

00:22:24.330 --> 00:22:26.897
All of these generous
unemployment benefits,

00:22:26.897 --> 00:22:30.833
all of people that are
on Medicaid and Medicare.

00:22:30.833 --> 00:22:34.770
These programs are gonna get
squeezed like never before

00:22:34.770 --> 00:22:37.390
because there's just
gonna be a total collapse

00:22:37.390 --> 00:22:38.802
in government revenue.

00:22:38.802 --> 00:22:41.940
And they're hoping that
the feds bill them out.

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:44.000
But the feds don't have any money either.

00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:45.552
So we're gonna print it.

00:22:45.552 --> 00:22:46.980
And there's all sorts of like,

00:22:46.980 --> 00:22:49.090
this would be a different
program about what's gonna happen

00:22:49.090 --> 00:22:50.261
when all that stuff happens.

00:22:50.261 --> 00:22:53.540
But everyone thinks that their resources

00:22:53.540 --> 00:22:56.008
are just like this
exogenous thing out there.

00:22:56.008 --> 00:22:59.569
That magic is there and all
we gotta do is redivide it.

00:22:59.569 --> 00:23:04.260
It now it's gonna disappear
and then we're gonna discover

00:23:04.260 --> 00:23:08.030
what that sort of social
justice model is gonna lead to.

00:23:08.030 --> 00:23:11.090
And it's gonna make everybody less safe.

00:23:11.090 --> 00:23:14.850
Everybody less healthy,
everybody more hungry.

00:23:14.850 --> 00:23:18.533
And I hope there's some
ownership of that, but we'll see.

00:23:19.370 --> 00:23:22.550
- So going on the assumption
there is an ownership of that

00:23:22.550 --> 00:23:24.699
because that doesn't seem
to be how inertia works.

00:23:24.699 --> 00:23:26.899
Or there might at least be states that

00:23:26.899 --> 00:23:28.640
sort of own it themselves.

00:23:28.640 --> 00:23:31.010
So you know, Florida and Texas for example

00:23:31.010 --> 00:23:33.930
with zero state income
tax are doing nice jobs,

00:23:33.930 --> 00:23:35.170
balancing their budgets.

00:23:35.170 --> 00:23:39.170
And I do sense there's sort
of idea floating around

00:23:39.170 --> 00:23:40.440
the internet right now.

00:23:40.440 --> 00:23:42.700
That in effect what
New York and California

00:23:42.700 --> 00:23:43.962
particularly are doing,

00:23:43.962 --> 00:23:47.776
is they're trying to absolutely
tank their own economies

00:23:47.776 --> 00:23:50.599
because they're so screwed.

00:23:50.599 --> 00:23:53.160
And I say this as someone
that grew up in New York

00:23:53.160 --> 00:23:54.800
and now lives in California.

00:23:54.800 --> 00:23:56.660
That they have so screwed themselves

00:23:56.660 --> 00:24:00.720
despite 13% income tax that
I pay here in California

00:24:00.720 --> 00:24:02.986
on top of LA you know, city tax.

00:24:02.986 --> 00:24:05.040
That they have no way of getting out.

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:08.020
So it's like destroy, let's
blow up the whole thing

00:24:08.020 --> 00:24:12.087
knowing the feds will have to
come in and clean up the mess.

00:24:12.087 --> 00:24:14.010
I know you're not for that,

00:24:14.010 --> 00:24:16.850
but do you think that
that's actually possible?

00:24:16.850 --> 00:24:19.580
That's really what like
Newsome's really sitting around

00:24:19.580 --> 00:24:21.050
going, we're looking at the numbers,

00:24:21.050 --> 00:24:22.540
there's no way out of this

00:24:22.540 --> 00:24:24.890
and let's just blow up the
whole damn thing and force...

00:24:24.890 --> 00:24:26.210
We'll stick it to Trump.

00:24:26.210 --> 00:24:27.043
Something like that.

00:24:27.043 --> 00:24:32.043
- Yeah, I think it's clearly
part of their strategy.

00:24:32.082 --> 00:24:35.930
They don't have any, they
don't seem to have any concern

00:24:35.930 --> 00:24:37.910
about actually cleaning
up their own house.

00:24:37.910 --> 00:24:40.220
So they're gonna look for that bailout.

00:24:40.220 --> 00:24:42.330
But let me, we talked
about conspiracy theories.

00:24:42.330 --> 00:24:43.424
Let me take it a step further.

00:24:43.424 --> 00:24:48.070
James Cliburn, who is
Nancy Pelosi's number three

00:24:48.070 --> 00:24:49.148
in the house.

00:24:49.148 --> 00:24:51.350
In early March when they were debating

00:24:51.350 --> 00:24:53.090
that first trillion-dollar bill,

00:24:53.090 --> 00:24:55.110
which turned into a $2.2 trillion bill.

00:24:55.110 --> 00:24:57.887
He said to his colleagues,

00:24:57.887 --> 00:25:01.589
"This is an opportunity to remake America

00:25:01.589 --> 00:25:05.400
"the way that we want to see it happen."

00:25:05.400 --> 00:25:07.970
And I think there's a
method to this madness.

00:25:07.970 --> 00:25:12.610
When you give people such
extravagant unemployment benefits

00:25:12.610 --> 00:25:14.610
that employers can't hire them back

00:25:14.610 --> 00:25:17.312
because they're making a ton
more money being unemployed.

00:25:17.312 --> 00:25:21.300
That sounds like something
that AOC would love to see

00:25:21.300 --> 00:25:23.545
because she told us all
not to go back to our jobs.

00:25:23.545 --> 00:25:26.190
- Yeah, she doesn't find
that there's no value

00:25:26.190 --> 00:25:27.650
in work basically.

00:25:27.650 --> 00:25:32.300
- Yeah, and we all know that
that's economically ignorance.

00:25:32.300 --> 00:25:36.167
But what if this is a
process to radically expand

00:25:36.167 --> 00:25:37.713
the welfare of state?

00:25:37.713 --> 00:25:41.365
Like if you're putting a
third of Americans out of work

00:25:41.365 --> 00:25:44.030
and you're giving them
these generous benefits,

00:25:44.030 --> 00:25:48.120
at what point is any
Republican or Democrat

00:25:48.120 --> 00:25:48.953
kind of roll that back?

00:25:48.953 --> 00:25:50.882
They never get rolled back.

00:25:50.882 --> 00:25:53.508
Temporary programs become permanent.

00:25:53.508 --> 00:25:55.770
But you know, the other piece of this,

00:25:55.770 --> 00:25:57.550
which I think is even more ominous,

00:25:57.550 --> 00:26:01.900
is this tracking system
that they're developing.

00:26:01.900 --> 00:26:05.460
Where if you do decide
to go to our restaurant,

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:07.440
there's gonna be some
government bureaucrat.

00:26:07.440 --> 00:26:10.099
They're wanting your
information so that they can get

00:26:10.099 --> 00:26:12.200
in contact with you later.

00:26:12.200 --> 00:26:14.450
That sounds like something the
communist Chinese would do.

00:26:14.450 --> 00:26:16.020
And yet all this--

00:26:16.020 --> 00:26:18.130
- If we want to do the conspiracy thing,

00:26:18.130 --> 00:26:21.219
then it's like, wow, maybe
China unleash this knowing all

00:26:21.219 --> 00:26:24.410
these stupid things that we
would then do to ourselves.

00:26:24.410 --> 00:26:28.280
- Well, it is sort of, I don't know if

00:26:28.280 --> 00:26:33.280
they did it on purpose, but if
we do adopt the Chinese model

00:26:33.726 --> 00:26:37.810
based on, by the way,
huge government failures

00:26:37.810 --> 00:26:39.496
that originated in China.

00:26:39.496 --> 00:26:41.670
One government failure after another,

00:26:41.670 --> 00:26:44.006
and all of a sudden we're
like, let's be like China

00:26:44.006 --> 00:26:46.160
then America's done.

00:26:46.160 --> 00:26:48.800
Like we're just done if
we're not gonna push back.

00:26:48.800 --> 00:26:51.950
But to your point, like the only way

00:26:51.950 --> 00:26:54.461
that the governor of California
and the governor of New York

00:26:54.461 --> 00:26:57.700
are held accountable
is if the people do it.

00:26:57.700 --> 00:26:59.090
And it's gotta be bottom-up.

00:26:59.090 --> 00:27:00.906
The same logic that applies to markets,

00:27:00.906 --> 00:27:04.590
applies to political pressure.

00:27:04.590 --> 00:27:05.840
And I'm not talking about voting,

00:27:05.840 --> 00:27:09.550
I'm talking about the people
that drive public opinion

00:27:09.550 --> 00:27:12.586
that actually shape what
politicians will do tomorrow.

00:27:12.586 --> 00:27:15.440
That's where this revolution has to start.

00:27:15.440 --> 00:27:18.926
It's gotta be peaceful it's
gotta be civilly disobedient.

00:27:18.926 --> 00:27:22.070
And you're seeing it in the streets

00:27:22.070 --> 00:27:23.710
and on the beaches today.

00:27:23.710 --> 00:27:25.217
People are like, "We're done.

00:27:25.217 --> 00:27:26.307
"You guys lied to us.

00:27:26.307 --> 00:27:28.258
"You told us that we're
flattening the curve

00:27:28.258 --> 00:27:30.590
"and now you won't let
us out of our houses.

00:27:30.590 --> 00:27:32.260
We're just done."

00:27:32.260 --> 00:27:34.133
- All right Kibbe, so
then I want some advice

00:27:34.133 --> 00:27:36.210
as a guy that lives in California.

00:27:36.210 --> 00:27:37.800
I mean, I've been tweeting about this

00:27:37.800 --> 00:27:41.170
and Fox News ran a Chiron I didn't realize

00:27:41.170 --> 00:27:43.377
they were going to when I
was on Dana Perino that said,

00:27:43.377 --> 00:27:45.750
"Dave Rubin, is considering
leaving California

00:27:45.750 --> 00:27:46.910
because of a tweet."

00:27:46.910 --> 00:27:49.400
But I do sense that the
rubbers meeting the road

00:27:49.400 --> 00:27:50.380
with my ideas.

00:27:50.380 --> 00:27:51.590
I believe in federalism.

00:27:51.590 --> 00:27:53.520
I believe in all the
things you're talking about

00:27:53.520 --> 00:27:55.040
and state's rights and the foot vote

00:27:55.040 --> 00:27:56.530
and all of those things.

00:27:56.530 --> 00:27:58.860
Do you think it's worth it
in a place like California

00:27:58.860 --> 00:28:01.290
for liberty-minded people and let's say,

00:28:01.290 --> 00:28:03.343
right-leaning people, whatever that is,

00:28:03.343 --> 00:28:06.440
they stay in fight or is
this thing too far gone?

00:28:06.440 --> 00:28:08.607
What do you actually think?

00:28:08.607 --> 00:28:12.140
- I think there's a very strong argument

00:28:12.140 --> 00:28:14.920
for fleeing these States
that are so authoritarian.

00:28:14.920 --> 00:28:17.580
And by the way, I should own up the fact

00:28:17.580 --> 00:28:19.059
I live in the district of Columbia,

00:28:19.059 --> 00:28:22.387
and my mayor is every bit as
authoritarian as your governor.

00:28:22.387 --> 00:28:25.239
We're locked down until June as well.

00:28:25.239 --> 00:28:29.040
Here and she's not releasing
the data, the modeling--

00:28:29.040 --> 00:28:30.801
- No data no nothing.
- And she thinks she is good.

00:28:30.801 --> 00:28:34.347
She says she has it but she
won't tell us what it is.

00:28:34.347 --> 00:28:36.210
So I'm in the same boat as you.

00:28:36.210 --> 00:28:38.660
I'm living literally in
the belly of the beast.

00:28:38.660 --> 00:28:40.283
I'm two blocks from the Capitol right now

00:28:40.283 --> 00:28:43.070
and I've been trying to convince my wife

00:28:43.070 --> 00:28:44.871
to let me flee to a red state.

00:28:44.871 --> 00:28:48.640
I actually think there's going
to be a political realignment

00:28:48.640 --> 00:28:50.920
that's not so much red versus blue.

00:28:50.920 --> 00:28:54.100
It's gonna be governors
that had the humility

00:28:54.100 --> 00:28:58.221
to let free people rebuild the mess

00:28:58.221 --> 00:29:00.251
that our governments have made.

00:29:00.251 --> 00:29:02.780
And people are gonna
vote with their feets.

00:29:02.780 --> 00:29:04.410
This is why we like federalism

00:29:04.410 --> 00:29:06.749
because there is
competition between states.

00:29:06.749 --> 00:29:09.520
I think Texans are a little worried

00:29:09.520 --> 00:29:11.989
that too many of you
Californians are gonna move there

00:29:11.989 --> 00:29:14.721
and break everything that they've done.

00:29:14.721 --> 00:29:17.010
- Well, that's where everybody, you know,

00:29:17.010 --> 00:29:18.950
people in Colorado are worried about that.

00:29:18.950 --> 00:29:21.310
People in Arizona are worried about that.

00:29:21.310 --> 00:29:22.261
That seems to happen.

00:29:22.261 --> 00:29:23.990
California's raised taxes

00:29:23.990 --> 00:29:25.589
on themselves, the state crumbles,

00:29:25.589 --> 00:29:27.840
and then they export these idiotic ideas.

00:29:27.840 --> 00:29:32.680
I'd almost be for Texas having
some sort of test to get in.

00:29:32.680 --> 00:29:34.060
I know they can't really do that,

00:29:34.060 --> 00:29:35.871
like some sort of like,
you can't come here

00:29:35.871 --> 00:29:39.090
to suddenly institute a state income tax

00:29:39.090 --> 00:29:39.923
or something like that.

00:29:39.923 --> 00:29:41.840
But all right, so with
that in mind though,

00:29:41.840 --> 00:29:43.720
for people that believe in federalism

00:29:43.720 --> 00:29:46.961
that believe that the way we
set this thing up was right.

00:29:46.961 --> 00:29:48.750
If what you're saying is right,

00:29:48.750 --> 00:29:50.010
and I've been thinking about this a lot,

00:29:50.010 --> 00:29:52.880
it's almost like, not to
say we're gonna end up

00:29:52.880 --> 00:29:53.810
in a civil war.

00:29:53.810 --> 00:29:58.250
But we are gonna then have to
actually watch states fail.

00:29:58.250 --> 00:30:03.013
That's an almost unimaginable
idea for a lot of people,

00:30:03.013 --> 00:30:04.210
don't you think?

00:30:04.210 --> 00:30:07.470
- Yeah, I mean, it's
gonna be very difficult.

00:30:07.470 --> 00:30:11.200
I could count on one and maybe two hands

00:30:11.200 --> 00:30:14.330
the number of Republicans
that I think have sort of

00:30:14.330 --> 00:30:17.365
the testicular fortitude to say no

00:30:17.365 --> 00:30:19.990
to bailing out California.

00:30:19.990 --> 00:30:21.931
And you know who those guys are.

00:30:21.931 --> 00:30:24.130
And there aren't very many others

00:30:24.130 --> 00:30:25.270
that are willing to do that.

00:30:25.270 --> 00:30:29.378
And so much about politics
is driven by their fear

00:30:29.378 --> 00:30:31.370
of not getting reelected,

00:30:31.370 --> 00:30:33.383
their fear that someone's
gonna call them a bad person

00:30:33.383 --> 00:30:35.430
because they didn't do that.

00:30:35.430 --> 00:30:37.258
But if you actually
want federalism to work,

00:30:37.258 --> 00:30:41.466
you can't bail out states
that have behaved so badly.

00:30:41.466 --> 00:30:44.627
You know, California was broke
long before this pandemic

00:30:44.627 --> 00:30:46.929
and everything they've done since then

00:30:46.929 --> 00:30:50.960
has only exacerbated their
ability to grow their way

00:30:50.960 --> 00:30:53.857
out of it once we get past the pandemic.

00:30:53.857 --> 00:30:56.470
It's gotta be held accountable.

00:30:56.470 --> 00:30:58.536
And this gets back to
this political realignment

00:30:58.536 --> 00:31:00.320
that I'm thinking about.

00:31:00.320 --> 00:31:02.947
I was, you know this, I
was a tea party organizer

00:31:02.947 --> 00:31:07.360
in 2008 when Nancy Pelosi and John Boehner

00:31:07.360 --> 00:31:10.927
walked down the aisle of
the house floor and said,

00:31:10.927 --> 00:31:12.764
"We must bail out Wall Street together."

00:31:12.764 --> 00:31:14.900
And the American people rose up

00:31:14.900 --> 00:31:16.780
because they had access
to information online.

00:31:16.780 --> 00:31:20.152
That to me was the day that I
think the tea party was born

00:31:20.152 --> 00:31:23.180
in opposition for the
Wall Street bailouts.

00:31:23.180 --> 00:31:24.720
And by the way, in the beginning,

00:31:24.720 --> 00:31:26.750
it wasn't a partisan thing at all.

00:31:26.750 --> 00:31:29.787
Occupy wall street had some
of the same predilections

00:31:29.787 --> 00:31:33.543
and some of the revolts
against the injustices of that.

00:31:33.543 --> 00:31:38.135
I think the lockdowns are
that same seismic shock

00:31:38.135 --> 00:31:40.044
on our political alignment

00:31:40.044 --> 00:31:42.410
and we'll see where the chips fall.

00:31:42.410 --> 00:31:45.179
But I think there's an
opportunity out of this disaster

00:31:45.179 --> 00:31:47.720
to get people to pay a
little more attention

00:31:47.720 --> 00:31:49.650
to what the government's doing to them.

00:31:49.650 --> 00:31:51.960
- It's interesting
because as you're saying

00:31:51.960 --> 00:31:53.970
that you'd love to convince
your wife to get moving

00:31:53.970 --> 00:31:55.917
and you know, we're having
those same discussions here.

00:31:55.917 --> 00:31:58.780
I'm kind of with you on
what you just said there,

00:31:58.780 --> 00:32:01.118
which is maybe this is the shock.

00:32:01.118 --> 00:32:05.247
Maybe the average Californian is gonna go,

00:32:05.247 --> 00:32:07.987
"Whoa, this just does not make sense.

00:32:07.987 --> 00:32:10.760
"What have we done,"
now that's putting maybe

00:32:10.760 --> 00:32:12.700
a little too much faith
in people waking up?

00:32:12.700 --> 00:32:14.960
But that's the part of me
that wants to stay in fight

00:32:14.960 --> 00:32:16.100
because I do love it here.

00:32:16.100 --> 00:32:17.627
That's the part that's like,

00:32:17.627 --> 00:32:19.020
"All right, dig in for,"

00:32:19.020 --> 00:32:21.213
you know, we try it for
like two, three more years

00:32:21.213 --> 00:32:25.296
to see if in the aftermath this
thing can rebuild properly.

00:32:25.296 --> 00:32:27.816
I don't know that it's possible, you know,

00:32:27.816 --> 00:32:32.663
maybe that's a little to
rose-colored glasses or something.

00:32:33.810 --> 00:32:35.330
- Well, I'm a libertarian.

00:32:35.330 --> 00:32:37.449
That has been most of my
career in Washington DC.

00:32:37.449 --> 00:32:42.213
So I'm perfectly building
chase impossible rainbows.

00:32:43.100 --> 00:32:48.100
- Yeah, if the states actually
do go these separate ways,

00:32:48.811 --> 00:32:51.373
do you think we have enough
of a national ethos anymore

00:32:51.373 --> 00:32:53.993
to keep us together as a nation?

00:32:55.356 --> 00:32:57.912
'Cause that seems like the
next step down the road

00:32:57.912 --> 00:32:58.810
with this, right?

00:32:58.810 --> 00:33:03.810
- Yeah, it's become increasingly worrisome

00:33:04.141 --> 00:33:07.492
the way with this tribal divide

00:33:07.492 --> 00:33:09.897
and how everything is political now.

00:33:09.897 --> 00:33:12.860
And it's always red versus blue

00:33:12.860 --> 00:33:15.435
even for most of us who
don't even identify with

00:33:15.435 --> 00:33:17.333
either one of those tribes.

00:33:17.333 --> 00:33:22.333
And that the authoritarian
left us so western libertarian

00:33:23.460 --> 00:33:25.196
like me away from it.

00:33:25.196 --> 00:33:28.270
We're all looking for a home

00:33:28.270 --> 00:33:31.620
and I still think that
classical liberalism

00:33:31.620 --> 00:33:34.100
or libertarianism or humility.

00:33:34.100 --> 00:33:36.020
Just call it the kind of humility

00:33:36.020 --> 00:33:37.429
that your mom tried to teach you.

00:33:37.429 --> 00:33:40.523
That to me is the only thing
that holds us together.

00:33:40.523 --> 00:33:44.640
Because if we're going
to use the reins of power

00:33:44.640 --> 00:33:46.243
at the state level, at the federal level

00:33:46.243 --> 00:33:49.220
to force everybody to
live just the way that

00:33:49.220 --> 00:33:50.405
we believe they should live.

00:33:50.405 --> 00:33:54.408
I don't see how that doesn't
get violent at some point

00:33:54.408 --> 00:33:56.770
because the alternative has to

00:33:56.770 --> 00:33:59.687
be peaceful cooperation, tolerance.

00:33:59.687 --> 00:34:02.460
You live your life exactly how you want,

00:34:02.460 --> 00:34:04.236
as long as you don't
hurt me or take my stuff.

00:34:04.236 --> 00:34:07.810
There is a solution here and
that solution, by the way,

00:34:07.810 --> 00:34:10.697
was very much built into
the American blueprint

00:34:10.697 --> 00:34:14.020
with the declaration of
independence and the bill of rights

00:34:14.020 --> 00:34:14.920
in the constitution.

00:34:14.920 --> 00:34:17.383
So we're gonna have to revisit that.

00:34:18.580 --> 00:34:22.394
And yeah, getting back
to this counterrevolution

00:34:22.394 --> 00:34:24.380
that we hope will happen.

00:34:24.380 --> 00:34:26.170
It's gotta start there because that is the

00:34:26.170 --> 00:34:27.594
only solution that keeps us together.

00:34:27.594 --> 00:34:30.340
- Yeah, because I could
also see a situation

00:34:30.340 --> 00:34:32.850
where the red state people,
so now you're in Texas,

00:34:32.850 --> 00:34:35.210
you know you have your
no state income tax,

00:34:35.210 --> 00:34:37.490
you still have federal income tax.

00:34:37.490 --> 00:34:38.327
But you're going, wait a minute,

00:34:38.327 --> 00:34:41.104
"Why am I paying all this money to the fed

00:34:41.104 --> 00:34:44.000
"to bail out the guys that who
screwed up the whole thing?"

00:34:44.000 --> 00:34:45.658
So I could see an average Texan

00:34:45.658 --> 00:34:48.280
who's living their life,
doing everything properly,

00:34:48.280 --> 00:34:50.864
following the law, voting
in the right type of people.

00:34:50.864 --> 00:34:54.732
Becoming seriously resentful
of people in another state,

00:34:54.732 --> 00:34:56.610
and that that's deeply dangerous.

00:34:56.610 --> 00:34:58.330
You mentioned classical
liberalism of course.

00:34:58.330 --> 00:34:59.880
So I have to ask you the question

00:34:59.880 --> 00:35:02.100
that every single
interviewer I think asked me

00:35:02.100 --> 00:35:04.310
on my book tour.

00:35:04.310 --> 00:35:06.697
Which is do you see any
meaningful distinction

00:35:06.697 --> 00:35:10.910
between classical liberalism
and libertarianism?

00:35:10.910 --> 00:35:13.279
Does it even matter or is
this just for the wonky guys

00:35:13.279 --> 00:35:15.804
to sit around after
libertarian conferences,

00:35:15.804 --> 00:35:18.456
have a drink, and whittle it away?

00:35:18.456 --> 00:35:21.068
- Well, of course, there's
only one perfect libertarian

00:35:21.068 --> 00:35:24.360
and that's me and everybody else.

00:35:24.360 --> 00:35:25.880
That's a libertarian joke, but right.

00:35:25.880 --> 00:35:30.443
Because we spend so much time
arguing about the minutia.

00:35:30.443 --> 00:35:33.720
So I actually don't think
there's a difference.

00:35:33.720 --> 00:35:38.720
And I could, depending on
who I'm trying to speak to,

00:35:39.310 --> 00:35:41.310
and I don't think there's
any contradiction in this.

00:35:41.310 --> 00:35:43.200
I either use the word libertarian,

00:35:43.200 --> 00:35:44.642
which Hayek says is a made-up word.

00:35:44.642 --> 00:35:46.468
He likes the word liberal.

00:35:46.468 --> 00:35:49.760
But now we have to qualify it
by saying classical liberal

00:35:49.760 --> 00:35:51.537
so that people appreciate
that it's different

00:35:51.537 --> 00:35:53.760
than how it might be used today.

00:35:53.760 --> 00:35:55.937
But I think constitutional conservative

00:35:55.937 --> 00:35:57.722
is kinda the same thing

00:35:57.722 --> 00:36:00.808
because we all may have
personal preferences

00:36:00.808 --> 00:36:03.853
and lifestyle choices that
we make that are different.

00:36:03.853 --> 00:36:07.797
But in terms of governance
and how we cooperate

00:36:07.797 --> 00:36:10.221
with each other, it's the same philosophy.

00:36:10.221 --> 00:36:13.566
It's all about that
localism that bottom-up.

00:36:13.566 --> 00:36:15.900
There's a couple rules, don't hurt people,

00:36:15.900 --> 00:36:17.240
don't take their stuff.

00:36:17.240 --> 00:36:19.760
Other than that, we all get along.

00:36:19.760 --> 00:36:21.699
And if you want to go
to church, go to church.

00:36:21.699 --> 00:36:24.430
If you don't want to go to
church, don't go to church.

00:36:24.430 --> 00:36:26.920
I don't really care because I'm minding

00:36:26.920 --> 00:36:30.210
enough of my own business
the way my mom taught me to.

00:36:30.210 --> 00:36:32.020
- Yeah, no, I'm with you on that.

00:36:32.020 --> 00:36:33.790
So I'm gonna ask you one or two sorts of

00:36:33.790 --> 00:36:35.643
the core libertarian things.

00:36:36.680 --> 00:36:38.710
Where are you at with open borders?

00:36:38.710 --> 00:36:40.143
This is one of the ones where

00:36:40.143 --> 00:36:42.830
when I talk to the more
libertarian minded people

00:36:42.830 --> 00:36:45.520
like I am completely
not an open borders guy.

00:36:45.520 --> 00:36:46.800
I get some of the arguments.

00:36:46.800 --> 00:36:48.660
I'd be happy for you to lay
out some of the arguments

00:36:48.660 --> 00:36:49.500
if you are.

00:36:49.500 --> 00:36:51.846
But this is one of the ones
where I think especially now

00:36:51.846 --> 00:36:56.500
I think the more nationalists,
and I don't mean nationalists

00:36:56.500 --> 00:36:57.790
in a scary word, nationalists.

00:36:57.790 --> 00:36:59.910
I believe we're nations and
we can set our own borders

00:36:59.910 --> 00:37:00.750
and things like that.

00:37:00.750 --> 00:37:03.000
I think Trump has some wins on that

00:37:03.000 --> 00:37:06.780
coming in the election
because of Coronavirus,

00:37:06.780 --> 00:37:08.610
our ability to control our own borders.

00:37:08.610 --> 00:37:10.360
But I know a lot of
libertarians don't like that.

00:37:10.360 --> 00:37:13.590
They want free trade,
free complete ability

00:37:13.590 --> 00:37:15.426
to bounce across everywhere all the time.

00:37:15.426 --> 00:37:16.785
Where are you at on that?

00:37:16.785 --> 00:37:19.584
- So I used to be for open borders,

00:37:19.584 --> 00:37:21.484
but now I've sort of changed my position

00:37:21.484 --> 00:37:24.167
because I do think we need to build a wall

00:37:24.167 --> 00:37:26.515
between California and America.

00:37:26.515 --> 00:37:27.399
(laughs loudly)

00:37:27.399 --> 00:37:30.330
Because it's very dangerous.
- Can I just get a date

00:37:30.330 --> 00:37:32.010
on that 'cause gonna get myself--

00:37:32.010 --> 00:37:35.260
- Yeah, but you know, I used to,

00:37:35.260 --> 00:37:37.772
I have a more libertarian
view on you than this.

00:37:37.772 --> 00:37:41.890
I used to give speeches,
to tea party audiences,

00:37:41.890 --> 00:37:44.830
I talked to progressive audiences
and my view is the same.

00:37:44.830 --> 00:37:46.603
If you want to come to
this country and work,

00:37:46.603 --> 00:37:50.540
and follow the rules we want you.

00:37:50.540 --> 00:37:53.810
And I don't think you need to build a wall

00:37:53.810 --> 00:37:55.660
to enforce a rule like that.

00:37:55.660 --> 00:37:59.420
What you need to do is get
politics out of the process

00:37:59.420 --> 00:38:01.760
of getting the people here that wanna work

00:38:01.760 --> 00:38:03.288
and wanna follow the rules.

00:38:03.288 --> 00:38:05.378
Because right now the system,

00:38:05.378 --> 00:38:08.420
these games there are some Republicans

00:38:08.420 --> 00:38:09.628
that don't want any immigration.

00:38:09.628 --> 00:38:13.350
There are some Democrats that
don't want any immigration

00:38:13.350 --> 00:38:16.152
because it undermines union power.

00:38:16.152 --> 00:38:18.950
And there are other Democrats that

00:38:18.950 --> 00:38:21.770
once people get here illegally,
they want to sort of own

00:38:21.770 --> 00:38:23.090
and control them.

00:38:23.090 --> 00:38:24.590
So the whole thing is super political.

00:38:24.590 --> 00:38:27.845
And I think the answer's
got to be a simplification

00:38:27.845 --> 00:38:31.067
of the process so that if
guest workers want to come

00:38:31.067 --> 00:38:32.868
and they wanna follow the rules,

00:38:32.868 --> 00:38:37.868
and they want to and or if
they wanna become a citizen

00:38:38.250 --> 00:38:39.410
and be an American.

00:38:39.410 --> 00:38:40.790
I think those processes should

00:38:40.790 --> 00:38:43.830
be predictable and unpolitical.

00:38:43.830 --> 00:38:45.610
And I think if you did those things,

00:38:45.610 --> 00:38:49.187
you could focus border
security on violent gangs

00:38:49.187 --> 00:38:53.108
that are running drugs
across the Mexican border.

00:38:53.108 --> 00:38:54.701
You could repeal a drug war,

00:38:54.701 --> 00:38:56.500
which would also get rid of the two gangs.

00:38:56.500 --> 00:38:58.988
There's all sorts of
free-market things you could do

00:38:58.988 --> 00:39:01.630
that I think would solve
some of the concerns

00:39:01.630 --> 00:39:04.670
that conservatives have
about border security

00:39:04.670 --> 00:39:06.340
without building a wall.

00:39:06.340 --> 00:39:08.560
So I am more libertarian on that,

00:39:08.560 --> 00:39:10.801
but I don't think it's unreasonable

00:39:10.801 --> 00:39:15.254
because of so many of the things
that do make America great

00:39:15.254 --> 00:39:18.489
come from immigrants
that came to this country

00:39:18.489 --> 00:39:22.226
and added value and built
this beautiful quilt

00:39:22.226 --> 00:39:23.560
that is America.

00:39:23.560 --> 00:39:25.251
So I don't wanna shut that down.

00:39:25.251 --> 00:39:26.790
- Yeah, and actually it doesn't sound

00:39:26.790 --> 00:39:28.460
like we're very far off on that.

00:39:28.460 --> 00:39:31.620
I always say I'm not sort of
for a wall or against a wall.

00:39:31.620 --> 00:39:33.678
If you told me there were
certain parts of the border

00:39:33.678 --> 00:39:37.330
that we could prove a wall
would be particularly effective.

00:39:37.330 --> 00:39:39.014
And there have been some places like that,

00:39:39.014 --> 00:39:40.320
then I'm okay with that.

00:39:40.320 --> 00:39:43.990
But I don't like the idea of,
you know, the 3000-mile wall,

00:39:43.990 --> 00:39:47.270
just so that we have
this idea of protection

00:39:47.270 --> 00:39:49.360
when in many ways we're
not gonna be doing that.

00:39:49.360 --> 00:39:51.690
And then, you know, I'm sorta, well,

00:39:51.690 --> 00:39:53.447
where are you at on like
pathway to citizenship

00:39:53.447 --> 00:39:56.750
for the 12 million or
so people that are here.

00:39:56.750 --> 00:39:58.670
What do you, do you put them
back at the back of the line

00:39:58.670 --> 00:40:00.797
or how do we even get these
guys to come out of the shadows

00:40:00.797 --> 00:40:02.050
and the rest of it?

00:40:02.050 --> 00:40:07.050
- Yeah, I mean, I think
that I would be for

00:40:07.474 --> 00:40:10.302
a pathway of citizenship
and it's easy to say that

00:40:10.302 --> 00:40:12.740
and I think politics would screw it up.

00:40:12.740 --> 00:40:14.476
But I think a better way
to solve that problem

00:40:14.476 --> 00:40:17.568
is that a lot of people
that came here illegally,

00:40:17.568 --> 00:40:21.209
probably would prefer a
reliable guest-worker program

00:40:21.209 --> 00:40:24.620
so they could go back home
and be with their families.

00:40:24.620 --> 00:40:27.048
I have lots of friends in
the free market community

00:40:27.048 --> 00:40:30.462
in Latin America, in
Honduras for instance.

00:40:30.462 --> 00:40:33.887
And I hear the story
again and again and again,

00:40:33.887 --> 00:40:38.429
that the reason that people are
illegal in the United States

00:40:38.429 --> 00:40:40.174
is that they don't have the confidence

00:40:40.174 --> 00:40:42.830
that they could come
and go with the seasons,

00:40:42.830 --> 00:40:44.940
say they're picking crops
or whatever they're doing,

00:40:44.940 --> 00:40:46.310
they can't do that.

00:40:46.310 --> 00:40:49.700
So I feel like the path
to citizenship thing

00:40:49.700 --> 00:40:52.371
is almost a changing of the conversation

00:40:52.371 --> 00:40:55.143
because we're primarily
talking about people.

00:40:55.143 --> 00:40:57.789
They just wanna do work that's
actually available to them

00:40:57.789 --> 00:41:01.080
and they're doing it
because their economies

00:41:01.080 --> 00:41:02.579
are so screwed up in Honduras.

00:41:02.579 --> 00:41:05.940
But the American drug war
has given all the power

00:41:05.940 --> 00:41:06.773
to the drug gang.

00:41:06.773 --> 00:41:10.450
So families are making that
march because they're desperate,

00:41:10.450 --> 00:41:11.889
it's not a choice they would make.

00:41:11.889 --> 00:41:15.945
So again, I think there's
probably a freedom-based solution

00:41:15.945 --> 00:41:20.350
that doesn't reward illegal behavior,

00:41:20.350 --> 00:41:24.794
but would get people
to voluntarily go back

00:41:24.794 --> 00:41:27.200
if there was a predictable
process by which

00:41:27.200 --> 00:41:28.190
they could come back and forth

00:41:28.190 --> 00:41:30.966
based on employment opportunities.

00:41:30.966 --> 00:41:34.530
- So where are you
generally at with Trump?

00:41:34.530 --> 00:41:36.890
Who I think the name
Trump was only mentioned

00:41:36.890 --> 00:41:38.923
once in this entire
interview, just a moment ago.

00:41:38.923 --> 00:41:43.640
I sense with most libertarians,
they kinda like a lot of

00:41:43.640 --> 00:41:46.355
the stuff Trump has done,
regulation cut taxes.

00:41:46.355 --> 00:41:48.980
We don't seem to be going to extra wars.

00:41:48.980 --> 00:41:51.440
If anything we're trying to
get out of Afghanistan now

00:41:51.440 --> 00:41:53.090
and we're not gonna be nation-building.

00:41:53.090 --> 00:41:54.503
That's all very libertarian stuff.

00:41:54.503 --> 00:41:57.879
I think a lot of libertarians
and one night we were at,

00:41:57.879 --> 00:41:59.710
I think it was a Yale conference

00:41:59.710 --> 00:42:01.400
and we had a table of libertarians.

00:42:01.400 --> 00:42:04.970
And it seemed very obvious
to me that everyone's sort of

00:42:04.970 --> 00:42:07.819
like the things he was doing
but just had a personal

00:42:07.819 --> 00:42:10.200
they just don't like him personally.

00:42:10.200 --> 00:42:11.690
Like it was that idea.

00:42:11.690 --> 00:42:14.680
And I sense that a lot of
people have that thing.

00:42:14.680 --> 00:42:16.040
Is that kinda where you are?

00:42:16.040 --> 00:42:17.800
What do you make of this
whole thing with Trump?

00:42:17.800 --> 00:42:19.540
Trump and the Liberty-minded people

00:42:19.540 --> 00:42:21.900
'cause obviously he's not his core is not

00:42:21.900 --> 00:42:24.520
a Liberty-minded core, he
would love to take more power

00:42:24.520 --> 00:42:25.670
if he could,

00:42:25.670 --> 00:42:29.218
but yet it seems like he's
doing a pretty decent job.

00:42:29.218 --> 00:42:31.690
- You know he has, he does have a sort of

00:42:31.690 --> 00:42:36.050
an authoritarian mindset
and I'm not even convinced

00:42:36.050 --> 00:42:37.405
he's ever read the bill of rights.

00:42:37.405 --> 00:42:41.620
So I guess that's an
excuse, but full disclosure,

00:42:41.620 --> 00:42:42.453
and you mentioned this.

00:42:42.453 --> 00:42:44.880
I did work for Rand Paul super back

00:42:44.880 --> 00:42:46.730
when he was running for president

00:42:46.730 --> 00:42:50.310
and then I ran a Gary
Johnson Super Pac after that.

00:42:50.310 --> 00:42:54.020
So as a candidate I was a never Trumper

00:42:54.020 --> 00:42:56.780
because I had a candidate
and his name wasn't Trump.

00:42:56.780 --> 00:42:59.453
As president, it's a
totally different thing.

00:42:59.453 --> 00:43:03.989
And I'll go back to when
Barack Obama was president.

00:43:03.989 --> 00:43:08.989
I spent a lot of my time
organizing the tea party

00:43:09.393 --> 00:43:13.750
and opposing the stimulus and
the bailouts and Obamacare

00:43:13.750 --> 00:43:15.070
and all that stuff.

00:43:15.070 --> 00:43:16.926
But when he wanted to do
criminal justice reform,

00:43:16.926 --> 00:43:20.200
I went to the justice department
and worked with Eric Holder

00:43:20.200 --> 00:43:22.000
in hopes to get something done.

00:43:22.000 --> 00:43:23.923
My attitude with Trump
is kind of the same way.

00:43:23.923 --> 00:43:27.636
When he's good, I wanna work with him.

00:43:27.636 --> 00:43:31.450
I've done lots of shows supporting him

00:43:31.450 --> 00:43:33.248
on things like criminal justice reform,

00:43:33.248 --> 00:43:36.793
staying out of so many permanent wars,

00:43:36.793 --> 00:43:39.857
and stuff like that that he's done.

00:43:39.857 --> 00:43:44.618
But on other things that
libertarians care a bunch about

00:43:44.618 --> 00:43:48.510
you know, we just had a
vote on surveillance power

00:43:48.510 --> 00:43:51.226
and whether or not the deep
state would get more power.

00:43:51.226 --> 00:43:53.640
Trump's been horrible on that.

00:43:53.640 --> 00:43:55.846
And I don't know if it's
bad advisors or what,

00:43:55.846 --> 00:43:59.484
if you care about dept and spending

00:43:59.484 --> 00:44:01.140
Trump's been horrible on that.

00:44:01.140 --> 00:44:06.038
So it's a mixed bag, but our
job when we have a president

00:44:06.038 --> 00:44:07.461
is to make them better.

00:44:07.461 --> 00:44:10.150
And so I don't spend all my
time railing about Trump.

00:44:10.150 --> 00:44:13.372
I'm hoping that he will
do some of the things

00:44:13.372 --> 00:44:15.550
that would make more sense.

00:44:15.550 --> 00:44:17.100
And I was hopeful, for instance,

00:44:17.100 --> 00:44:21.225
that he would be a strong
voice on reopening the economy.

00:44:21.225 --> 00:44:24.820
But he's been a little
hard to understand on this.

00:44:24.820 --> 00:44:26.210
He spent a lot of time

00:44:26.210 --> 00:44:28.867
picking on the Governor of Georgia, Kemp,

00:44:28.867 --> 00:44:32.410
who was one of the first to
unlock down their economy.

00:44:32.410 --> 00:44:35.686
So even on that, it's
a mixed bag with Trump.

00:44:35.686 --> 00:44:38.500
But you know, I'm a libertarian.

00:44:38.500 --> 00:44:39.650
I don't really get on board

00:44:39.650 --> 00:44:44.650
with big government whether
Republican or Democrat.

00:44:45.104 --> 00:44:47.190
- You mean you don't think Trump can solve

00:44:47.190 --> 00:44:48.480
all of your personal problems?

00:44:48.480 --> 00:44:50.160
Is that what you're telling me?

00:44:50.160 --> 00:44:51.490
- I kind of want to live in a world where

00:44:51.490 --> 00:44:54.410
I don't have to worry
who the president is.

00:44:54.410 --> 00:44:55.610
That's how naive I am.

00:44:55.610 --> 00:44:57.627
Like if you had a president.

00:44:57.627 --> 00:44:59.782
- (laughs loudly) Yeah, I'm with you man.

00:44:59.782 --> 00:45:00.615
- Yeah.

00:45:02.500 --> 00:45:04.140
- All right, so let's move off Trump.

00:45:04.140 --> 00:45:06.410
You mentioned Rand Paul and
since you did work with him

00:45:06.410 --> 00:45:07.243
for a little bit.

00:45:07.243 --> 00:45:09.290
I had him on last week, and
one of the things that I said

00:45:09.290 --> 00:45:11.180
to him was sort of exactly
what you just laid out

00:45:11.180 --> 00:45:12.370
there about Trump.

00:45:12.370 --> 00:45:15.200
Which is that one of the
reasons I'm more okay

00:45:15.200 --> 00:45:18.780
now with Trump is that
Rand Paul has become

00:45:18.780 --> 00:45:20.120
one of his biggest allies.

00:45:20.120 --> 00:45:22.490
And, it's interestingly, 'cause
Paul's explanation of why

00:45:22.490 --> 00:45:24.530
he's okay with Trump is
sort of what you just said

00:45:24.530 --> 00:45:26.300
that yeah, there's good, there's bad,

00:45:26.300 --> 00:45:28.598
but I can try to push him the
best I can on the good stuff

00:45:28.598 --> 00:45:29.670
and the rest of it.

00:45:29.670 --> 00:45:31.450
So in a weird way, it
almost seems to be that

00:45:31.450 --> 00:45:34.290
that's the best libertarians can do.

00:45:34.290 --> 00:45:38.201
We're not particularly good at
getting candidates out there,

00:45:38.201 --> 00:45:41.610
but you know, the party is
just an absolute disaster.

00:45:41.610 --> 00:45:43.170
You want to explain a little
bit about why the party

00:45:43.170 --> 00:45:45.950
seems to be such a disaster.

00:45:45.950 --> 00:45:48.550
Even though so many of the
ideas if you could get people

00:45:48.550 --> 00:45:50.400
to listen to them, they are right.

00:45:50.400 --> 00:45:52.680
And again, maybe sort of getting
us back to the beginning.

00:45:52.680 --> 00:45:55.123
Maybe they are about
to have a real moment.

00:45:56.100 --> 00:46:00.880
- Yeah, so personally I just
wanna out that both Rand Paul

00:46:00.880 --> 00:46:03.770
and Mike Lee are in a
highly leveraged position

00:46:03.770 --> 00:46:04.603
in the Senate.

00:46:04.603 --> 00:46:07.400
And they on any given vote,
they could be the difference

00:46:07.400 --> 00:46:09.630
between passing and not passing.

00:46:09.630 --> 00:46:11.949
And I think they both
done a very good job.

00:46:11.949 --> 00:46:15.160
You know, neither one of
them obviously Rand Paul

00:46:15.160 --> 00:46:18.921
ran against Trump and Mike
Lee supported Ted Cruz

00:46:18.921 --> 00:46:20.589
in the primaries,

00:46:20.589 --> 00:46:24.482
but they've sort of have
stepped up and said,

00:46:24.482 --> 00:46:28.420
"Mr. President, I'm gonna work
with you on the things that

00:46:28.420 --> 00:46:31.080
we share in common and I'm
gonna try to convince you

00:46:31.080 --> 00:46:31.940
on some other things."

00:46:31.940 --> 00:46:33.824
And I think that's a
really smart thing to do.

00:46:33.824 --> 00:46:35.960
And libertarians that criticized that

00:46:35.960 --> 00:46:38.700
I don't really understand
because the point is

00:46:38.700 --> 00:46:39.650
to get the right policy.

00:46:39.650 --> 00:46:41.833
The point's not to sort of virtue signal

00:46:41.833 --> 00:46:44.015
that Trump's an awful guy.

00:46:44.015 --> 00:46:46.288
And but for the libertarian party

00:46:46.288 --> 00:46:48.340
and third parties, generally,

00:46:48.340 --> 00:46:52.030
I think that there is
inevitably a moment where

00:46:52.030 --> 00:46:54.820
the two-party duopoly does break up.

00:46:54.820 --> 00:46:58.400
And the reason I think
that the libertarian party

00:46:58.400 --> 00:47:00.500
has struggled so much is that

00:47:00.500 --> 00:47:03.060
it is the two parties that set the rules

00:47:03.060 --> 00:47:05.547
by which political parties operate.

00:47:05.547 --> 00:47:08.840
And Gary Johnson was a
great example of that.

00:47:08.840 --> 00:47:10.950
They kept changing the
rules of how you got

00:47:10.950 --> 00:47:12.810
onto the presidential stage.

00:47:12.810 --> 00:47:14.860
By any historical measure,
Gary Johnson would have been

00:47:14.860 --> 00:47:16.220
on that stage.

00:47:16.220 --> 00:47:18.040
But they keep changing the
rules and that makes it

00:47:18.040 --> 00:47:20.730
very difficult for parties to compete.

00:47:20.730 --> 00:47:22.419
Because if you're raising
money for politics,

00:47:22.419 --> 00:47:25.694
the value pitch that you make to donors is

00:47:25.694 --> 00:47:27.515
we can win this race.

00:47:27.515 --> 00:47:30.986
For a third party and
not just libertarians

00:47:30.986 --> 00:47:34.344
but greens or
constitutionalists or whatever.

00:47:34.344 --> 00:47:38.640
The two parties that
control all the power,

00:47:38.640 --> 00:47:40.774
make it virtually impossible
for them to compete.

00:47:40.774 --> 00:47:45.045
And it's usually some accident
like say Abraham Lincoln,

00:47:45.045 --> 00:47:48.153
that creates a new third party.

00:47:49.140 --> 00:47:51.720
It's gonna happen but it's kind
of a chicken and egg thing.

00:47:51.720 --> 00:47:55.020
The Libertarian Party
would have ballot access

00:47:55.020 --> 00:47:56.400
in all 50 States.

00:47:56.400 --> 00:47:57.938
Would have a better donor base,

00:47:57.938 --> 00:48:02.570
would attract more talent if
they were allowed to compete

00:48:02.570 --> 00:48:04.730
and I dunno how that breaks through.

00:48:04.730 --> 00:48:06.620
They gotta breakthrough somehow.

00:48:06.620 --> 00:48:08.820
- Yeah, so you're saying
it's not the naked guy

00:48:08.820 --> 00:48:10.577
running on stage that's
gonna breakthrough,

00:48:10.577 --> 00:48:12.263
is that what you're saying?

00:48:13.128 --> 00:48:16.240
- (laughs loudly) So
when I was raising money

00:48:16.240 --> 00:48:18.840
for Gary Johnson, I had brought on

